-- Seed: COMPLETE coverage for every code a home-based palliative
-- practice could plausibly bill, across all three published fee schedules.
--
-- Supersedes the two earlier narrow seeds (payer-rules-state-fee-schedules
-- -2026.sql and payer-rules-medicare-rvu26c.sql, 16 hand-picked codes
-- each). Those left every other code answering "Unknown", which is the
-- same failure as an empty library, only narrower. This is a strict
-- superset produced by the same deterministic logic.
--
-- SCOPE: 99202-99499 (the whole E/M family), G01xx-G04xx (care
-- management, home-health certification, prolonged services), and
-- 36415/36416 (venipuncture, routinely drawn at a home visit).
-- 770 distinct code/schedule facts -> 2886 rules after fan-out.
--
-- SOURCES, all read DETERMINISTICALLY from cells and columns — no LLM,
-- so source_quote is the row as published:
--   Ohio     Appendix DD to rule 5160-1-60, rev. 01/01/2026
--   NC       Physician Services Fee Schedule (Provider Specialty 001)
--   Medicare CMS National PFS Relative Value File RVU26C (July 2026)
--
-- DECISION RULES, each validated and two of them corrected after review:
--   * Ohio payment cells are not always numeric. NC/D mean not payable;
--     B (bundled) and BR (by report) mean the service IS payable and are
--     rendered 'varies', never not_covered — reading them as non-coverage
--     would tell a biller to drop revenue they are owed.
--   * The Ohio status code cited in each answer is the cell that was
--     actually read. An earlier version hardcoded "status code 3" and
--     shipped rows whose own quote said "status 2".
--   * Medicare status codes are CMS's own (Attachment A, RVU26C.pdf).
--     Only A/R/T RVUs are used for payment. CMS's caveat on "A" is
--     carried into the answer: payable under the fee schedule is NOT a
--     national coverage determination.
--   * 99343 is never emitted. It is absent from the CMS file and Ohio
--     lists it discontinued from 01/01/2023; NC still prices it, which
--     is stale.
--
-- FAN-OUT AND ITS LIMIT. A state Medicaid schedule is the state's
-- coverage determination and a Medicaid plan must cover what the state
-- covers, so it fans to that state's Medicaid plans only. Commercial
-- plans (Anthem Ohio, Humana Ohio, Aetna, Medical Mutual) are excluded —
-- Ohio Medicaid has no authority over them and asserting coverage would
-- be invention. Medicare is federal, so it is written once per state.
--
-- Stored rates are the STATE FFS / CMS NATIONAL maximum, labelled as such
-- in the answer; a plan's contracted rate and a locality's GPCI-adjusted
-- amount both differ.
--
-- Every answer passed a self-consistency gate: any status code, payment
-- code or dollar amount it claims is present in its own source_quote.
--
-- Idempotent: deterministic UUIDs + ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE.

BEGIN;

DO $$
BEGIN
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM payer WHERE id = 'a0000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000401'::uuid) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Traditional Medicare (Part B) payer row is missing. Apply db/seed/payer-medicare.sql first.';
  END IF;
END $$;

